   $q$Ji-woo is copying $\angle ABC$ onto a ray with endpoint $E$. Ji-woo draws an arc centered at $B$ that crosses the sides of the angle at $D$ and $F$, then an arc with the same width centered at $E$ that crosses the ray at $G$. What should Ji-woo do next?$q$, $q$[{"text":"draw arcs centered at $D$ and $F$ to bisect $\\angle ABC$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-with-angle-bisection"},{"text":"draw a line from $E$ through point $D$ of the original angle","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"connected-to-original-figure"},{"text":"set the compass to the distance $DF$ and draw an arc centered at $G$ crossing the arc through $G$","is_correct":true},{"text":"set the compass to the distance $BD$ and draw an arc centered at $G$ crossing the arc through $G$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"copied-radius-instead-of-chord"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$set the compass to the distance $DF$ and draw an arc centered at $G$ crossing the arc through $G$$q$, $q$The copy must match the opening of the angle, which is measured by the chord $DF$, so the compass is set to $DF$ and swung from $G$. Using $BD$ instead copies the radius, which always makes a $60^\circ$ angle no matter what $\angle ABC$ is.$q$, 'draft', null)
