   $q$In a circle with center $O$, Mateo draws diameter $\overline{AC}$. Mateo then constructs the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{AC}$, which meets the circle at $B$ and $D$. What figure is $ABCD$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"a square","is_correct":true},{"text":"an equilateral triangle","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-with-triangle-construction"},{"text":"a regular hexagon","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-with-hexagon-construction"},{"text":"a rectangle that is not a square","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-diagonals-unequal"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$a square$q$, $q$The diagonals $\overline{AC}$ and $\overline{BD}$ are both diameters, so they are equal, they bisect each other, and they are perpendicular, which makes $ABCD$ a square. Equal diagonals alone would allow a rectangle, but the right angle at $O$ makes all four sides equal too.$q$, 'draft', null)
