   $q$To draw a line through point $P$ parallel to line $\ell$, Sofia draws a transversal through $P$ that meets $\ell$ at $Q$. Sofia then copies the angle formed at $Q$ to point $P$ in the corresponding position. Why is the new line parallel to $\ell$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"vertical angles are congruent","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"cited-vertical-angles"},{"text":"alternate interior angles are supplementary","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-supplementary-and-congruent"},{"text":"congruent corresponding angles mean the lines are parallel","is_correct":true},{"text":"both lines are perpendicular to the transversal","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-right-angle"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$congruent corresponding angles mean the lines are parallel$q$, $q$When a transversal makes congruent corresponding angles with two lines, the lines are parallel, and copying the angle makes them congruent. The angle at $Q$ need not be a right angle, so the lines are not both perpendicular to the transversal.$q$, 'draft', null)
