   $q$In a random sample of 100 shoppers, $40\%$ prefer paper bags. To judge the variability, a student simulates 1000 random samples of 100 from a population with $40\%$ preferring paper. The simulated sample proportions are approximately normal with mean $0.40$ and standard deviation $0.05$. Using two standard deviations, what is the margin of error?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$0.05$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-one-standard-deviation"},{"text":"$0.025$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"halved-the-sd"},{"text":"$0.15$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-three-standard-deviations"},{"text":"$0.10$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$0.10$$q$, $q$About $95\%$ of simulated proportions fall within $2\sigma$ of the center, so the margin of error is $2(0.05) = 0.10$, giving plausible values from $0.30$ to $0.50$. The choice $0.05$ uses only one standard deviation.$q$, 'draft', null)
