   $q$Rafael claims that $n^3 - n$ is divisible by 6 for every integer $n$. Which factorization best explains why?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$n(n - 1)^2$, a product that contains a square","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrote-perfect-square"},{"text":"$(n - 1)(n^2 + n + 1)$, a difference of cubes","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misapplied-cube-identity"},{"text":"$n(n^2 - 1)$, a product of two even numbers","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-factors-are-even"},{"text":"$(n - 1)n(n + 1)$, a product of three consecutive integers","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$(n - 1)n(n + 1)$, a product of three consecutive integers$q$, $q$$n^3 - n = n(n^2 - 1) = (n - 1)n(n + 1)$, and among three consecutive integers one is a multiple of 2 and one is a multiple of 3, so the product is a multiple of 6. The form $(n - 1)(n^2 + n + 1)$ equals $n^3 - 1$, not $n^3 - n$.$q$, 'draft', null)
