   $q$For $0 \le \theta < 2\pi$, which angles satisfy $\cos\theta = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{11\\pi}{6}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-negative-sign"},{"text":"$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-reference-angle"},{"text":"$\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-cosine-with-sine"},{"text":"$\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi}{6}$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\frac{5\pi}{6}$ and $\frac{7\pi}{6}$$q$, $q$Cosine is negative in quadrants II and III, and the reference angle for $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is $\frac{\pi}{6}$, so $\theta = \pi - \frac{\pi}{6} = \frac{5\pi}{6}$ or $\pi + \frac{\pi}{6} = \frac{7\pi}{6}$. The choice $\frac{2\pi}{3}$ and $\frac{4\pi}{3}$ gives cosine $-\frac{1}{2}$, from the wrong reference angle.$q$, 'draft', null)
