   $q$Oluwaseun encodes a pair of numbers $p$ for a puzzle by computing $Ep$ with $E = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 5 & 2 \end{bmatrix}$. His friend receives the coded vector $\begin{bmatrix} 21 \\ 38 \end{bmatrix}$. What was the original pair?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\begin{bmatrix} 101 \\\\ 181 \\end{bmatrix}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-by-code-matrix"},{"text":"$\\begin{bmatrix} -148 \\\\ 93 \\end{bmatrix}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-on-wrong-side"},{"text":"$\\begin{bmatrix} 25 \\\\ -29 \\end{bmatrix}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-to-swap-diagonal"},{"text":"$\\begin{bmatrix} 4 \\\\ 9 \\end{bmatrix}$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 9 \end{bmatrix}$$q$, $q$$\det E = 6 - 5 = 1$, so $E^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -5 & 3 \end{bmatrix}$ and $E^{-1}\begin{bmatrix} 21 \\ 38 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 42 - 38 \\ -105 + 114 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 9 \end{bmatrix}$. The choice $\begin{bmatrix} 101 \\ 181 \end{bmatrix}$ multiplies by $E$ again instead of undoing it with $E^{-1}$.$q$, 'draft', null)
